With normal autocmds on neovim and using `sed`, it would probably look like this:

```
-- Commands when Vim start
vim.api.nvim_create_autocmd("VimEnter", {
  command = ":<nick> !sed -i -e 's/{ left = 20, right = 20, top = 15, bottom = 15 }/{ left = 0, right = 0, top = 0, bottom = 0 }/g' '<add your wezterm config file path here>'",
})

-- Commands when just before Vim exits
vim.api.nvim_create_autocmd("VimLeavePre", {
  command = ":<nick> !sed -i -e 's/{ left = 0, right = 0, top = 0, bottom = 0 }/{ left = 20, right = 20, top = 15, bottom = 15 }/g' '<add your wezterm config file path here>'",
})
```

Sorry, maybe I did something wrong and this didn't work. I also tried to directly change the padding of wezterm to 0, and then started neovim, but the bottom edge was still not filled.
```lua
return {
  -- Commands when Vim start
  vim.api.nvim_create_autocmd("VimEnter", {
    command = ":<nick> !sed -i -e 's/{ left = 5, right = 5, top = 5, bottom = 0 }/{ left = 0, right = 0, top = 0, bottom = 0 }/g' 'C:/Users/<user>/.config/wezterm/config/appearance.lua'",
  }),

  -- Commands when just before Vim exits
  vim.api.nvim_create_autocmd("VimLeavePre", {
    command = ":<nick> !sed -i -e 's/{ left = 0, right = 0, top = 0, bottom = 0 }/{ left = 5, right = 5, top = 5, bottom = 0 }/g' 'C:/Users/<user>/.config/wezterm/config/appearance.lua'",
  }),
}

I tried this approach and it worked, but there were still unfilled areas at the bottom and on the right.
```lua
vim.api.nvim_create_autocmd("VimEnter", {
  command = ":e C:/Users/<user>/.config/wezterm/config/appearance.lua | %s/{ left = 5, right = 5, top = 5, bottom = 0 }/{ left = 0, right = 0, top = 0, bottom = 0 }/g | w",
})
vim.api.nvim_create_autocmd("VimLeavePre", {
  command = ":e C:/Users/<user>/.config/wezterm/config/appearance.lua | %s/{ left = 0, right = 0, top = 0, bottom = 0 }/{ left = 5, right = 5, top = 5, bottom = 0 }/g | w",
})
```

@participant5 I think this is not a good solution. The solution I use now is to select a good-looking background image in wezterm, and then choose a theme with good transparency support in Neovim. Similar to this
![image](https://github.com/participant2/wezterm/assets/149876001/3d960a41-7278-4141-8e81-6cac32ae9bb3)
And then, @participant2 I have to mention the transparency setting. I tried to test it on my friend's computer. The wezterm, configuration file, and operating system are the same. The only difference is the hardware. However, my low-end screen cannot display the transparency effect. Adjusting the transparency parameter can only darken the background.
